L_REPORT", "source_category": "Annual Report for FY ended 31 March 2020", "uploaded_date": "FY ended 31 March 2020", "source_path": "https://cdn.cse.lk/cmt/upload_report_file/688_1601284578937.pdf", "discovered_at": "2026-07-09T18:24:47.249545", "source_type": "manual"}, {"symbol": "TKYO", "company_name": "TOKYO CEMENT COMPANY (LANKA) PLC", "doc_type": "ANNUAL_REPORT", "source_category": "Annual Report as at 31st March 2026", "uploaded_date": "08 Jul 2026 03:39:43 PM", "source_path": "https://cdn.cse.lk/cmt/upload_report_file/688_1783505383015.pdf", "discovered_at": "2026-07-09T17:26:05.423598", "source_type": "automated"}], "summary": {"symbol": "TKYO", "company_name": "TOKYO CEMENT COMPANY (LANKA) PLC", "generated_at": "2026-07-09T13:08:11.558858+00:00", "summary_text": "Tokyo Cement manufactures and sells cement in Sri Lanka -- the country's largest cement producer and its only listed one -- earning through volume (tonnage) x price per bag, sold through a dealer/distribution network (Annual Report 2023/24, Chairman's Message; Annual Report 2021/22, p.31). The single biggest dependency is construction-sector demand, which is itself gated by government infrastructure spending and lending rates (Annual Report 2023/24, Management Discussion and Analysis). Group revenue swung from LKR 35.8Bn (FY2020) up to LKR 56.5Bn (FY2023) and back down to LKR 49.8Bn (FY2024), tracking construction-cycle demand rather than a steady growth trend. Profitability has been considerably more volatile than revenue: FY2022 was a reported Group loss (LKR -0.36Bn) driven by a large exchange loss following the 2022 Rupee float, even though revenue grew 22% that year (Annual Report 2021/22, Chairman's Message) -- a clear illustration of currency/import-cost exposure sitting on top of the underlying cement business.", "gaps_note": "FY2025 (year ended 31 March 2025) not included: the FY2025 annual report exists in the document library (collected live, source_id 51996) but was not read for this p